A||||8||88formula||I need info on the 84 fire recall||||1||
Z||000000||88formula||12-14-2000||08:32 PM||||I'm sick and tired of close minded ignorant people saying to me "fiero's are fire traps". I would like some information on how many 84 fiero's actually burned because of the bad connecting rods so I can come up with a rebuttal. I remember reading somewhere that about 100 out of 120,000 units built in 84 actually burned because of the bad connecting rods. That is about .08% of the 84 fiero's on the road, which is almost insignificant. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||12-15-2000||06:22 PM||||1600 members and know one knows!||||reg||1
Z||000002||bubbajoexx||12-15-2000||08:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88formula:<BR><B>1600 members and know one knows!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>i dont know how many burned but I can tell you that it was not connecting rod failure<BR>it was a defect in the oil drain back holes in the block that was the droblem the oil did not return to the pan fast enough and the crank starved for oil causing the rodbearing tofail and putting a rod though the block the remaining oil spiled out onto the hot converter causing a fire. I had one of the aflicked fieros in 84 and gm replaced the engine . It only had 1000klms on it when it blew up lucky it was winter the snow saved the car from the usual fire that ocures.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||12-15-2000||08:49 PM||||There was more than just one reason for the '84 fires. Both of you are correct. <BR>The connecting rods used in '84 were bad, I think it was 1 in 10 rods were defective(4 rods per engine, you can do the math for likelyhood of a bad rod in an engine). Second, the '84 ran a quart low to begin with. With oil not able to drain back fast enough the engine was starved for oil. 3rd lack of owner maintanance was the final blow for many of the fires. When Fiero oil level was allowed to drop, the engine was really beginning to run low, couple low oil level, bad rods, and a bad oil passage design, you're asking for fire. I have documents I can scan if you would like. I can dig them out, they even give an exact number of Fiero fires.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||12-15-2000||09:14 PM||||fiero56 I am looking for statistics on the number of fiero's that actually burned because of the defective engines. If those documents you have list the information that I am looking for than could you send them to me via e-mail?<P>Thank you.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||12-15-2000||09:32 PM||||Found it.<P>1 and 4 of every 10 rods procudec were defective. Hairline cracks define "defective".<P>Some engine block castings were bad, allowing for oil and/or coolant to leak under normal conditions.<P>Radiator fans were wired backwards.<P>Radiator hoses had been installed improperly at the factory allowing for leaks.<P>By the end of 1985 1200 Fieros had caught fire. 1 in every 1700 flamed up by the end 1985. By middle of August 1986 reports were so common they caught the attention of the NHTSA. Don't have a final count for fires, but more than most Fiero enthusiasts want to believe.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||12-15-2000||10:58 PM||||a summary of sorts for the 4 cyl fire recal is in my cave. <A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Fiero Cave</A><P>The full detail is in ALLdata. I don't have the stats on how many. just what was involved in the fix.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||12-16-2000||03:59 AM||||i heard that roughly 600 out of the first 60,000 made actually started on fire because of the bad connecting rods.||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||12-16-2000||03:25 PM||||Thanks guys that was what I was looking for.||||reg||1
